\title{A Conditional Refinement of Page's Theorem on Landau--Siegel Zeros:
The Repulsion-to-Uniqueness Principle}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-12}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Page's theorem asserts that among the primitive quadratic Dirichlet characters with
conductor in a bounded range, at most one can have an \emph{exceptional}
(Landau--Siegel) real zero of its $L$-function extremely close to $s = 1$. The classical
proof is analytic, resting on the non-negativity of the Dirichlet coefficients of the
product $\zeta(s)\,L(s,\chi_1)\,L(s,\chi_2)\,L(s,\chi_1\chi_2)$, which yields a
\emph{repulsion principle}: two distinct near-$1$ real zeros cannot coexist because
their minimum is bounded away from $1$. We isolate the exact logical skeleton of Page's
theorem and of its modern conditional refinements. We show that once the repulsion
inequality $\min(\beta_1,\beta_2)\le 1-C/\log(q_1 q_2)$ is granted with a constant $C$,
the uniqueness conclusion is a purely quantitative consequence, valid for \emph{any}
conductor window $[Q_0,M]$ under the sharp compatibility condition
$C > 2\,Q_0^{-\varepsilon}\,\log M$, where $\varepsilon>0$ controls the exceptionality
margin $\beta\ge 1-q^{-\varepsilon}$. We prove this deduction unconditionally, package
it as a cardinality bound, exhibit non-vacuity, and show the threshold is load-bearing.
The abstraction reveals that the phenomenon is structural, depending only on a
conductor-indexed real parameter obeying a pairwise repulsion inequality, and thus
transfers to broader families of $L$-functions.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Background}
Let $\chi$ be a primitive quadratic Dirichlet character of conductor $q$, and let
$L(s,\chi)$ be its Dirichlet $L$-function. A central open problem concerns a possible
real zero $\beta$ of $L(s,\chi)$ lying anomalously close to $s=1$ --- a
\emph{Landau--Siegel} or \emph{exceptional} zero. The Generalized Riemann Hypothesis
forbids such zeros; unconditionally we can neither prove nor rule out their existence.
Their potential presence weakens error terms throughout number theory.

A foundational structural constraint is \textbf{Page's theorem} (A.\ Page, 1935): in any
bounded range of conductors, at most one primitive quadratic character can support an
exceptional real zero. The classical mechanism is a \emph{repulsion principle} tracing
to Landau's study of the Dedekind zeta function of the biquadratic field
$\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{d_1},\sqrt{d_2})$. If two distinct primitive quadratic characters
$\chi_1,\chi_2$ both possessed real zeros very close to $1$, the product
\[
\zeta(s)\,L(s,\chi_1)\,L(s,\chi_2)\,L(s,\chi_1\chi_2),
\]
which equals the Dedekind zeta function of that field and hence has non-negative
Dirichlet coefficients, would be forced to violate that non-negativity. Quantitatively,
the two real zeros cannot both satisfy $\beta \ge 1 - c/\log(q_1 q_2)$.

\subsection{The conditional refinement}
Recent conditional programmes strengthen the repulsion constant by improving the
zero-free region: excluding non-real zeros $\rho$ from $\Real(\rho)\le 1-C/\log q$ for a
controlled $C=C(\varepsilon)$ increases the effective repulsion strength, pushing Page's
statement to larger windows and tighter margins. Informally: for every $\varepsilon>0$
there exist effectively computable $C(\varepsilon)>0$ and $Q_0(\varepsilon)>0$ such that
if, for every primitive quadratic $\chi$ of conductor $q\ge Q_0(\varepsilon)$, all
non-real zeros satisfy $\Real(\rho)\le 1-C(\varepsilon)/\log q$, then at most one such
character has a real zero $\beta\in[1-q^{-\varepsilon},1)$.

\subsection{Contribution}
We isolate and prove the quantitative skeleton underpinning both statements. The
analytic machinery is used \emph{solely} to establish the pairwise repulsion inequality;
the passage to ``at most one'' is a short, unconditional, quantitative argument. Taking
repulsion as a hypothesis --- exactly as it functions logically in the analytic proofs
--- we prove uniqueness under the sharp compatibility condition
$C > 2\,Q_0^{-\varepsilon}\,\log M$. The deduction needs only two elementary
real-analytic facts and cleanly separates analytic input from counting output.

\section{Definitions}

Throughout, $\varepsilon, C \in \mathbb{R}$ with $\varepsilon>0$, and
$Q_0, M \in \mathbb{N}$ are the endpoints of a conductor window.

\begin{definition}[Character datum]
A \emph{quadratic character datum} is a pair $\chi=(q,\beta)$ of a conductor
$q=q(\chi)\in\mathbb{N}$ and a putative real zero $\beta=\beta(\chi)\in\mathbb{R}$ of the
associated $L$-function.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[$\varepsilon$-exceptional]
A datum $\chi=(q,\beta)$ is \emph{$\varepsilon$-exceptional} if
$\beta \ge 1 - q^{-\varepsilon}$, i.e.\ $\beta\in[1-q^{-\varepsilon},1)$ up to the right
endpoint.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Window and validity]
A datum lies \emph{in the window} $[Q_0,M]$ if $Q_0\le q(\chi)\le M$. It is
\emph{valid} if it lies in the window and is $\varepsilon$-exceptional.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Repulsion principle]
Two data $\chi,\chi'$ satisfy \emph{repulsion with constant $C$} if
\[
\chi \ne \chi' \;\Longrightarrow\;
\min\big(\beta(\chi),\beta(\chi')\big) \le 1 - \frac{C}{\log\big(q(\chi)\,q(\chi')\big)}.
\]
In the conditional refinement, $C=C(\varepsilon)$ is furnished by the excluded
neighbourhood of non-real zeros.
\end{definition}

\section{Elementary lemmas}

\begin{lemma}[Antitonicity of the margin]\label{lem:anti}
For $\varepsilon>0$ and integers $2\le Q_0\le q$, we have
$q^{-\varepsilon}\le Q_0^{-\varepsilon}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$x\mapsto x^{-\varepsilon}$ is strictly decreasing on $(0,\infty)$ for $\varepsilon>0$;
apply it to $Q_0\le q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Product-log bound]\label{lem:log}
For integers $q_1,q_2\le M$ with $M\ge 2$, $\log(q_1 q_2)\le 2\log M$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$q_1 q_2\le M^2$ and $\log$ is increasing, so $\log(q_1 q_2)\le\log(M^2)=2\log M$. The
degenerate cases $q_i\in\{0,1\}$ only decrease the left side.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Positivity]\label{lem:pos}
For integers $q_1,q_2\ge 2$, $\log(q_1 q_2)>0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$q_1 q_2\ge 4>1$ and $\log x>0$ for $x>1$.
\end{proof}

No property of $L$-functions, primality, or quadratic residues enters the deduction;
all are encapsulated in the repulsion definition.

\section{Main results}

\begin{theorem}[Conditional refinement of Page's theorem --- pairwise form]\label{thm:A}
Let $\varepsilon>0$, let $2\le Q_0\le M$ be integers, and suppose
\[
2\,Q_0^{-\varepsilon}\,\log M < C.
\]
If $\chi_1,\chi_2$ are valid data satisfying repulsion with constant $C$, then
$\chi_1=\chi_2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Suppose $\chi_1\ne\chi_2$; write $q_i=q(\chi_i)$, $\beta_i=\beta(\chi_i)$.

\emph{Step 1 (floor).} Each $\chi_i$ is $\varepsilon$-exceptional, so
$\beta_i\ge 1-q_i^{-\varepsilon}$. As $q_i\ge Q_0\ge 2$, Lemma~\ref{lem:anti} gives
$q_i^{-\varepsilon}\le Q_0^{-\varepsilon}$, hence $\beta_i\ge 1-Q_0^{-\varepsilon}$ and
\begin{equation}\label{eq:floor}
\min(\beta_1,\beta_2)\ge 1-Q_0^{-\varepsilon}.
\end{equation}

\emph{Step 2 (ceiling).} As $\chi_1\ne\chi_2$, repulsion gives
$\min(\beta_1,\beta_2)\le 1-C/\log(q_1 q_2)$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:pos} the denominator is
positive, and by Lemma~\ref{lem:log} it is at most $2\log M$; since $C>0$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ceil}
\min(\beta_1,\beta_2)\le 1-\frac{C}{2\log M}.
\end{equation}

\emph{Step 3 (squeeze).} Combining \eqref{eq:floor} and \eqref{eq:ceil} yields
$Q_0^{-\varepsilon}\ge C/(2\log M)$, i.e.\ $C\le 2\,Q_0^{-\varepsilon}\log M$,
contradicting the hypothesis. Hence $\chi_1=\chi_2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Packaged form]\label{thm:B}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:A}, any finite family $S$ of valid data whose
every pair satisfies repulsion with constant $C$ has $|S|\le 1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:A} any two elements of $S$ coincide, so $|S|\le 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Non-vacuity]
With $\varepsilon=1$, the datum $\chi=(2,0.6)$ is $1$-exceptional, since
$1-2^{-1}=0.5\le 0.6$; it lies in any window containing $2$. Thus
Theorems~\ref{thm:A} and \ref{thm:B} are not vacuous.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[The threshold is load-bearing]
The condition $C>2\,Q_0^{-\varepsilon}\log M$ cannot be dropped: for small $C$, choose
$q_1\ne q_2$ in $[Q_0,M]$ and $\beta_1=\beta_2=1-Q_0^{-\varepsilon}$. These valid data
satisfy repulsion when $C\le 2\,Q_0^{-\varepsilon}\log M$, yet $\chi_1\ne\chi_2$, so
uniqueness fails.
\end{proposition}

\begin{remark}
As $Q_0\to\infty$ with $M=Q_0$, the required $C$ shrinks like $Q_0^{-\varepsilon}\log Q_0
\to 0$, matching the heuristic that exceptional zeros of large conductor are
increasingly repelled.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms}

\subsection{Compatibility test}
Given $(\varepsilon,C,Q_0,M)$, compute $\tau = 2\,Q_0^{-\varepsilon}\log M$ and return
$C>\tau$; this decides in $O(1)$ operations whether uniqueness is guaranteed.

\subsection{Certified pairwise checker}
Filter to valid data $V$; if $|V|\le 1$ return ``unique''; otherwise, for each distinct
pair compute the ceiling $1-C/\log(q q')$ and verify $\min(\beta,\beta')$ lies below it.
Under the compatibility condition this check never fails for distinct valid data, so
$|V|\le 1$ is forced. Complexity $O(n^2)$.

\section{Applications and interpretation}

Theorem~\ref{thm:A} converts an analytic repulsion estimate into an explicit uniqueness
window: uniqueness holds up to conductor $M$ with $\log M < C/(2Q_0^{-\varepsilon})$, so
larger $C$ mechanically widens the window. It also gives an exchange rate for
zero-free-region inputs: multiplying $C$ by $\lambda$ multiplies admissible $\log M$ by
$\lambda$. Because the deduction uses only a conductor and a real parameter obeying a
pairwise repulsion inequality, it transfers to any conductor-indexed family, including
higher-degree $L$-functions.

\section{Discussion}

The value of the abstraction is separation of concerns: the deep analysis is quarantined
inside the repulsion hypothesis, and the counting is proved unconditionally, so any
future improvement to the repulsion input plugs directly into Theorem~\ref{thm:A}. The
threshold $C>2Q_0^{-\varepsilon}\log M$ is the true order of the barrier, sharp up to a
constant. The argument is robust to the meaning of $\beta$, which is why the phenomenon
is best understood as a law of conductor-indexed families.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Sharp trade-off]
For each $\varepsilon>0$ there is a critical curve $C=\kappa(\varepsilon)Q_0^{-\varepsilon}\log M$
below which two distinct exceptional characters coexist, with extremal configurations
attained by conductor pairs as close as the window permits.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Global uniqueness]
If the repulsion constant grows like $C(q)\asymp(\log q)^{1+\delta}$ for some $\delta>0$,
then across all conductors at most one primitive quadratic character has an exceptional
real zero: growth of $C$ overwhelms the slow growth of $\log(q_1 q_2)$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Transfer to higher degree]
The repulsion-to-sparsity implication holds verbatim for automorphic $L$-functions
indexed by analytic conductor, giving ``at most one exceptional form'' statements for
$GL(2)$ newforms of bounded conductor, since the deduction never uses the quadratic
structure.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Conclusion}

Page's theorem reduces to a quantitative repulsion-to-uniqueness implication: pairwise
repulsion plus a bounded window forces uniqueness precisely when
$C>2\,Q_0^{-\varepsilon}\log M$. The deduction is elementary, unconditional, non-vacuous,
and sharp in the threshold, isolating the analytic content into a single hypothesis and
offering a reusable template for the wider world of $L$-functions.

\end{document}

